{"kind":"assert","text":"One condition of request 541ab735 is deliberately not met, and the request is wrong on that point.\n\nIt asks to stop naming a waited-on performer in withdrawn and stale rows. I implemented it, three tests failed, and reading them changed my mind rather than the tests.\n\nTestStaleCommitmentKeepsItsWaitingParty pins the opposite with a stated reason: staleness is a qualifier beside the status, not a replacement for it, and it does not release the party who still owes something. A promise whose basis moved is still a promise its maker owes. Clearing the waited-on party makes the row read as though nobody owes anything, which is a worse falsehood than the one the request was trying to remove — it turns \"someone still has to do this\" into \"this is nobody's problem\".\n\nThe withdrawn case turned out not to exist as described. A request retired before anyone promised has no performer at all, so nothing names one; there was nothing to remove.\n\nThe order matters for whoever reads this later. The request was filed before the stale-commitment qualifier work landed, and that work established the current behaviour deliberately after measuring what flattening a stale row to \"stale\" had hidden — a kept promise, and the requester who still owed it a ratification. So this is not the request being careless. It is a condition of satisfaction overtaken by a decision made later on better evidence, and the honest resolution is to say so rather than implement it and quietly weaken a test that was written to prevent exactly this.\n\nThe other two defects the request names are real, were reproduced against this log, and are fixed at a668051e. Five commitments in this workroom were showing an acknowledged completion as cancelled.\n\nRecording this durably because it changes a condition of satisfaction, and because a future reader comparing the request to the implementation would otherwise find a gap with no explanation and either re-open it or assume it was missed."}